VALUES ('800a85ad-8d6b-4bf7-b10b-7cdb76115e57', '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000400', 'Two good examples of infrastructure development are expansions made to the nation’s road network and the construction of the ‘Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam. The road network has grown from 18,000Km in 1991 to over 120, 000Km nowadays-a significant increase of 70% over 25 years. Despite the steady economic growth in the 1990s, followed by rapid, sustained, growth between 2004/5 to 2015, and a concurrent improvement in socio-economic conditions, the country remains one of the poorest in the world. About 25 million people, around a quarter of the population, still live below the poverty line (living on less than 1.25 dollars. Every year, before and after the economic boom of 2005, between 5 and 18 million Ethiopians have had to depend on food aid. Levels of food insecurity massively increased in 2015 when one of the country’s worst droughts occurred. Moreover, the development of the infrastructure and the economic growth registered are characterized by the lack of fair distribution among the regional states and the concentration of wealth among the few rich. The FDRE constitution itself is still contested for its content and how it came about. It was', 'G10-History-STB-2023-web.pdf p.1', '2026.1', 'published', '[0.021226425,0.022064034,0.033694364,-0.052889336,0.024614356,0.012918673,-0.012897152,-0.027291771,0.025339717,-0.10586064,-0.03893578,-0.012268251,0.013714033,0.041125987,-0.049150564,-0.01012468,0.007134319,-0.038073268,-0.0067390082,0.03426617,0.019173333,0.024112191,0.018112456,-0.015812125,-0.01846924,0.037134368,0.009940453,-0.038262848,-0.008336625,0.17684826,-0.018211663,-0.025494391,0.031598765,-0.022977894,0.036087483,-0.018425912,-0.009585379,-0.041977942,0.0004086897,-0.019750284,0.01086347,0.030573536,0.010748373,0.0058450396,-0.008547906,0.00092977326,-0.0155376615,0.032961257,-0.002384302,0.00071883335,-0.009441746,-0.031411823,0.06858357,-0.078928836,0.030